NAME
       builtins - commands built into bash

SYNOPSIS
       help [COMMAND]
       type COMMAND

DESCRIPTION
       Some commands are not separate programs but part of bash itself:
       they change the shell (cd, export, umask), control scripts (if-tests,
       loops, exit) or are simply faster that way (echo, printf). They have
       no manual pages of their own; this page lists them, and help COMMAND
       prints bash's short description of each one. type COMMAND tells
       whether a name is a builtin, an alias, a function or a program.

       Some builtins also exist as programs (echo, test, printf, kill, pwd).
       In a script, the builtin is used.

COMMANDS
   Moving around and the environment
       cd [DIR]       Change the current directory (cd alone: home; cd -:
                      the previous one).
       pwd            Print the current directory.
       pushd, popd, dirs
                      Keep a stack of directories.
       export NAME[=VALUE]
                      Pass a variable on to programs started from this shell.
       unset NAME     Remove a variable or function.
       declare, typeset, local, readonly
                      Create variables with attributes (local: only inside a
                      function; readonly: cannot change).
       umask [MODE]   Show or set the permission bits removed from new files
                      (umask 022: files 644, directories 755; umask 027:
                      files 640, directories 750). Lasts for this shell; put
                      it in ~/.bashrc to keep it.
       alias NAME='COMMAND', unalias NAME
                      Define or remove a short name for a command.
       ulimit         Show or set limits such as open files (ulimit -n).

   Output and input
       echo [-n] [-e] TEXT
                      Print TEXT (-n: no newline; -e: understand \n, \t).
       printf FORMAT [ARGS]
                      Print with a format (%s text, %d number, \n newline).
       read [-r] [-p PROMPT] [-s] NAME...
                      Read a line from standard input into variables (-s: do
                      not show it; -r: keep backslashes).
       mapfile, readarray
                      Read lines into an array.

   Running things
       source FILE, . FILE
                      Run FILE's commands in the current shell (so its
                      variables stay set).
       eval TEXT      Run TEXT as a command line.
       exec COMMAND   Replace the shell with COMMAND.
       command NAME   Run the program NAME, ignoring aliases and functions.
       time PIPELINE  Report how long a command took.
       type NAME      Say what NAME is (builtin, alias, file...).
       hash           Show or reset bash's memory of program paths.
       history        Show the command history (!N runs entry N again).

   Scripts
       if, then, elif, else, fi; for, while, until, do, done; case, esac
                      Keywords for conditions and loops.
       test EXPR, [ EXPR ]
                      Check a condition (see test(1)).
       exit [N]       Leave the shell or script with status N.
       return [N]     Leave a function.
       break, continue
                      Leave a loop, or go on with its next round.
       shift [N]      Drop the first N arguments ($2 becomes $1).
       getopts        Read -x style options in a script.
       let EXPR, (( EXPR ))
                      Arithmetic: let n=n+1, ((n++)).
       set [-e] [-u] [-x] [-o OPTION]
                      Shell options: -e stop on an error, -u unset
                      variables are errors, -x print each command before
                      running it (good for finding bugs), -o pipefail.
       shopt          Further shell options.
       trap COMMAND SIGNAL
                      Run COMMAND when the shell gets SIGNAL (or EXIT).
       true, false, : Do nothing and succeed (or fail).

   Jobs
       jobs           List the background jobs of this shell.
       fg [%N], bg [%N]
                      Bring a job to the foreground, or let a stopped one go
                      on in the background (Ctrl+Z stops a job).
       wait [PID]     Wait for background jobs.
       disown         Let a job keep running after the shell exits.
       kill [-SIGNAL] PID|%N
                      Send a signal (the builtin also takes %N job numbers).

   Information
       help [NAME]    Short help for the builtins.
       compgen, complete
                      Tab completion.
       enable         Turn builtins on or off.
       logout         Leave a login shell.

EXAMPLES
       Find out what a name is:

           type cd ls ll

       Make new files private for this session:

           umask 077

       Debug a script by watching each command:

           bash -x /root/myscript.sh

SEE ALSO
       bash(1), test(1), kill(1)
